.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:3:Revision 23, 2026-11-20, after D210 (changes requested by the owner on 2026-11-20, given in chat, after T12's forty-seventh review, verify-118, and T03's forty-sixth, verify-119). What changed from revision 22, keeping every id. D193 approved revision 22. Since then each task was reviewed eight more times, every review failed, and the owner took each over seven times (D194 to D207); D208 and D209, the unblocks the eighth round raised, are pending. verify-118 failed T12 at 43332c6 on a KeyboardInterrupt during a read-back across a replaced case lock. verify-119 failed T03 at 3ae10cc on a mapping key YAML cannot hash beside a repeated pattern, and on a repeated direction that leaves the bank's code debit-only in one version. D210 accepts such corners as edge cases outside the guarantee, to push development forward, and asks for two scope limits. R4 and R54 now say that a rule table that is not well formed is refused as a whole, naming the faults its loader finds, at least one, and that naming every fault, or every combination of a repeated key's values, is outside R4; a well-formed table is checked rule by rule as before. R42, R43 and P15 now say that removing, renaming or replacing a case lock file while the case is in use, and interrupting a save at an arbitrary instant, are outside their guarantees: the tool reports as it can, and a check after the interference stops gives the true answer. Revision 23 reads D210's 'as it can' as reaching the two guarantees R42 and P15 have stated under such interference since revision 13, and keeps R42's refusal of a save begun inside another; it asks the owner about both (Options considered). No code changes: both builds already do what revision 23 asks, and both latest majors fall outside it. The design takes in verify-104 to verify-119 and D193 to D210. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, the traceability table, Out of scope, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Security notes and Consumer trace.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:5:Revision 22, 2026-11-20, after D192 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's thirty-ninth review, verify-102, and T03's thirty-eighth, verify-103). What changed from revision 21, keeping every id. D169 approved revision 21. Between it and D192 each task was reviewed eleven times, every review failed, and the owner took each task over ten times (D170 to D189). R43 and P15 now say how a reader's check after its read looks at the lock files: at each one it counts on and then at the first again, and, for a reader that holds the case, also at whether each file it holds still has a name. They also state for readers the window R42 has stated for writers since revision 10: a lock file removed or replaced, renamed aside and replaced included, after the check's last look at it is outside what any check can know (D192, after verify-102; 523c5df). R4 now says that only a digest that matches the pin spares v1 the exception check. It also says that a key given more than once is a fault whose every value is still checked, the rules of every rules list and each value of a repeated field, whichever comes first (D192, after verify-103; 4c017d4). No other requirement changes; R42 already puts lock-file interference outside its guarantee. The owner built these before this revision, and neither commit is reviewed yet; D190 and D191, the unblocks, are pending. At 4c017d4 the compile check and the matcher build reach only the last value of a repeated field, and the checks for the pattern forms a v2 table refuses read none of its values (Context). D192's own words ask for every value, so T03 extends those checks before its review, and revision 22 asks no question. The design takes in verify-82 to verify-103 and D169 to D192. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, the traceability table, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout and Consumer trace items 17, 20, 23 and 24.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:7:Revision 21, 2026-11-20, after D168 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-eighth review, verify-80, and T03's twenty-seventh, verify-81). What changed from revision 20, keeping every id. D164 answered revision 20's question with its default: one answer per report, the take-back's refusal worded for the save; the owner built it in 7fb3a23. R42 now says that each report a save's writer gets gives one answer, its causes included: a not-confirmed report names what stopped its take-back in the save's own words, and no part of it says the save was recorded, was not recorded or that nothing was written (D164, D168, after verify-80). R42 also says that whether a save's head record was published is known from its rename, never inferred from a head record that may be unreadable, so a head record that cannot be read before its rename answers not recorded (D168, after verify-80; 26bc51e). R4 now says that only v1 as its bytes were verified is spared the exception check, so a changed v1 names its forbidden exceptions after the changed-table refusal, and is otherwise judged as the kind of table it says it is. It also says that every rule's matchers are built while the table is checked, so a pattern that cannot be used is named with the table's other faults (D168, after verify-81; 5f5bb2e). No other requirement changes: P15 asks less than R42 here, and R54 refers to R4 for what else a changed v1 breaks. The owner built these before this revision, and none is reviewed yet: T12's 26bc51e and 7fb3a23, and T03's 5f5bb2e. Revision 21 asks no question. At 7fb3a23 a save refused before any part of its line is in the log is told only that the step was refused, where R42 asks for not recorded. At 5f5bb2e an unusable pattern on a rule with another malformed field is still found only once that field is mended (Context). The design takes in verify-80 and verify-81 and D164 to D168. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, C5, the traceability table, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 20, 23 and 24.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:9:Revision 20, 2026-11-20, after D163 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-seventh review, verify-78, and T03's twenty-sixth, verify-79). What changed from revision 19, keeping every id. D159 answered revision 19's question with its default: the reads a save makes only to check something name an operating-system error, and a re-check that cannot read answers not recorded, never a conflict; the owner built it in 1e35352. R42 now names those reads: the comparisons of the log and the head record with the save's check, the read-back and the examination of the case lock files. It says that one that cannot be made is reported as its error, never as a conflict to reload or a lock file removed or replaced (D163). R42 and P15 now say that once any part of a save's line is in the log, no part of what its writer is told says nothing was written (D163, after verify-78; de6a56e). R4 now says that a malformed value is a fault of its rule whatever else the rule carries, the bank-code flag included. It also says that whatever a change to v1 makes fail, the refusal names the changed frozen table first (D163, after verify-79; 5139e22). No other requirement changes; R54 already says what R4 now adds for a changed v1. The owner built these before this revision, and none is reviewed yet: T12's de6a56e and 1e35352, and T03's 5139e22. At 1e35352 a save whose take-back is refused quotes the refusal in its not-confirmed report. With a case lock file replaced, the quoted words say nothing was written, which R42 and P15 now rule out. With the head record no longer the save's own, they say the save was not recorded, so revision 20 asks the owner whether a not-confirmed report may quote what stopped its take-back (Options considered). At 5139e22 no type error escapes the table checks, but a failure no check foresees is refused naming no rule (Context, T03's twenty-sixth review). The design takes in verify-78 and verify-79 and D159 to D163. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, C5, the traceability table, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 20, 23 and 24.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:11:Revision 19, 2026-11-20, after D158 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-sixth review, verify-76, and T03's twenty-fifth, verify-77). What changed from revision 18, keeping every id. D154 answered revision 18's question with its default: R42's answer for a save the file system fails reaches every step the save takes after its check, and 51c8b68 maps the steps that change a file. R42 now states that answer as one rule: every operating-system error a save meets after its check is reported, naming the error, as not recorded, or as not confirmed when the save's head record was published and not taken back (D158). It names the steps the rule covers, and counts among them the reads a save makes to re-check something or to read itself back. R42 and P15 now say that a writer refused once any part of its line is written reports its save as not recorded, never as nothing written, the part staying past the recorded entries as a torn entry (D158, after verify-76; 9c265a3). R4 and R54 now say that the table loaded as v1 is judged by its bytes before anything else in it is checked, with whatever else keeps it from loading named after the changed-table refusal, and R4 that a value the format does not allow in a rule's field is named by rule, never raised as a type error (D158, after verify-77; 7cb2e71). No other requirement changes. The owner built these before this revision, and none is reviewed yet: T12's 9c265a3 and 51c8b68, and T03's 7cb2e71. At 51c8b68 the reads a save makes only to check something still turn the file system's error into an answer that names nothing, and before the line into a conflict that asks the person to reload; R42 as revision 19 writes it rules that out, so revision 19 asks the owner whether R42's naming reaches those reads (Options considered). At 7cb2e71 one validator path still raises a type error, for a bank-code rule whose pattern is a list (Context, T03's twenty-fifth review). The design takes in verify-76 and verify-77 and D154 to D158. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, C5, the traceability table, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 20, 23 and 24.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:13:Revision 18, 2026-11-20, after D153 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-fifth review, verify-74, and T03's twenty-fourth, verify-75). What changed from revision 17, keeping every id. D149 answered revision 17's question with its default: the take-back re-checks both lock files before its rename, as every head rename does, and a take-back that re-check stops answers not confirmed; R42 has said so since revision 17, and 7c8fba2 builds it. R42 now also says that when the file system fails a step a save takes after its check, the save answers, naming the error, which never reaches the caller as itself: not recorded before the rename that publishes its head record, and not confirmed after it, when the head record cannot be made durable (D153, after verify-74). R43 now says that a read taken again goes through the gate and the case lock, so a busy-gate read whose case lock is removed during it looks again, never reading as a reader that finds no gate (D153, after verify-74). R4 now says that only the frozen v1 table, known by the hash pinned in code, is spared the check that no rule carries an exception, and that every other table, whether or not it calls itself frozen, is checked rule by rule and refused naming the rule (D153, after verify-75). The term not confirmed widens to its second cause. No other requirement changes, and P15, which asks less than R42 and R43 here, does not. The owner built these before this revision, and neither commit is reviewed yet: T12's 7c8fba2 and T03's 9042fb4. 7c8fba2 maps the file system's errors where the head record is published; at a save's other steps they still reach the caller as themselves, which R42 as revision 18 writes it rules out, so revision 18 asks the owner how far R42's clause should reach (Options considered). The design takes in verify-74 and verify-75 and D149 to D153. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, C5, the traceability table, Out of scope, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 20 and 23 and a new item 24.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:15:Revision 17, 2026-11-20, after D148 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-fourth review, verify-72, and T03's twenty-third, verify-73). What changed from revision 16, keeping every id. R4 now says that the set-aside rule is judged match by match, from the longest down: a match set aside inside a longer phrase sets nothing aside even where its rule counts by another match, and a rule counts where any match of its own counts (D142, the owner's answer to revision 16's first question). R42 and P15 now name a third answer for a save, not confirmed, when its read-back fails and its head record is not then taken back (D143). R43 and P15 now say which lock files each reader counts on, so that a holder's read and a busy-gate read count only while theirs are still the case's (D148, after verify-72). They also say that lock-file interference beyond a read across such a lock file is outside what a reader can know, and that a check made after it stops gives the true answer (D144). No other requirement changes. The owner built these before this revision, and none is reviewed yet: T03's 4b3b21c, and T12's 0d339cc and 3076547. D148 also asks R42 to say that the take-back checks only that the head record is still the save's own, as 0d339cc builds. Revision 17 does not write that clause: under lock-file interference it lets a take-back unrecord a repair that was told it was recorded, which R42's own guarantee rules out (Context, T12's twenty-fourth review). R42 keeps the take-back among the steps a writer re-checks both lock files before, a take-back that re-check stops answers not confirmed, and revision 17 asks the owner. The design takes in verify-72 and verify-73 and D142 to D148. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's verdict step 1, C3, C5, the traceability table, Out of scope, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 19, 20 and 23.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:432:**What the repo already does** (re-read at 43f3e57; the lines revision 1 cited are where it said). It imports a five-column CSV or a Monzo export with strict validation (src/cma/ingest.py, src/cma/importers/monzo.py). An ordered regex categoriser never guesses (src/cma/categorise.py). No rule mentions cash, so a cash deposit today falls to OTHER and shows in M16 (src/cma/categorise.py:37-50, 64-66). It computes 16 figures with evidence ids (src/cma/metrics.py:248-292); revenue is the set REVENUE_CATEGORIES (src/cma/models.py:33), which M2, M3, M4 and M9 read (src/cma/metrics.py:121, 172). It applies an illustrative YAML policy to pass, refer or fail (src/cma/policy.py:83-123, src/cma/policies/uk_sme_default.yaml), and the guard already accepts a threshold tagged [Rn] when it is introduced as a limit (src/cma/guard.py:189-201). A LangGraph pipeline drafts the memo, checks it with the guard and stops for a named reviewer (src/cma/graph.py). There is an offline template model (src/cma/llm.py:26-69) and an MCP server (src/cma/mcp_server.py).
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:434:On the T03 branch (cited at ecec4ca for revision 4; not merged) the v2 table is data with a stage and a protected flag on every rule, cash paid in is no longer protected, and the payment rail is a cue with no category (src/cma/rules/v2.yaml:117-122 and 214-236 on that branch). A credit's verdict is computed in two stages, and an ambiguous credit records `v2:conflict:` and its candidates (src/cma/categorise.py:389-404 and 446-462). Revision 4 found three gaps there: a cue with a bucket returned before the rail-plus-cash check (:399), the candidates came from the deciding stage only (:399, :403), and word boundaries counted only ASCII letters and digits (:121-126). The sixth attempt closed them and left the two that D64 settles. The T03 worktree (branch at b4b511c for revision 6, at 5bac88c for revisions 7 and 8, at 79965d4 for revision 9, at d507393 for revision 10, reviewed as verify-53, and at b6494fd for revisions 11 and 12, after D100) also holds D70-D72: the cheque is a line-type cue with no category, no rule carries an exception, advance wording sits on the loan list, and listed names and profile names share one separator set (src/cma/categorise.py:206-217 at b6494fd). Since the owner's D78 take-over, a profile name the wording matches joins an ambiguous credit's candidates instead of settling it. Since D86 every kind it matches joins, and a credit both a director's and an own account's names match is ambiguous whatever the table finds; since the D90 change (e24f466) such a credit records every rule it matched (src/cma/categorise.py:730-760 at b6494fd, :750). README.md:78 there still says the profile settles the credit. A protected verdict outranks a profile name there, for credits (:739-741) and for debits (:699-704), as C1 has said since revision 3 and R5 and R6 say since revision 8. Since D94-D97, the validator compiles every form of a pattern the matcher uses. It also refuses back-references and conditional groups, and allows the bank-code flag only on the rule whose whole pattern is a bank return code listed in code, with no names (:94-101, :253-321; src/cma/rules/v2.yaml:63-69). A rule's pattern and names are scanned apart, and at each start the longest match any of them, or any alternative inside a pattern, can make is kept (:557-611, D102). The bank-code rule is never counted as inside another rule's longer match (:625-627, D101). Both were verify-53's majors at d507393. The round-trip pass undoes only customer transfers, and M7, M15 and the M16 numerator read returned_dd, revenue_transfer and refund_in (src/cma/metrics.py:30, 156, 207), so the category a credit gets inside its bucket changes figures. Reversal linking compares payees after setting aside a fixed list of bank prefixes that has UNPAID DD but not RETURNED DD (src/cma/categorise.py:767-807). At 78556a7 (the owner's review-21 fix, not yet reviewed) the code has moved again: the table checks are validate_ruleset (:356-501), which refuses an exception on every v2 rule (:423-432) and a lookahead or lookbehind in a v2 pattern (:433-439, with _has_lookaround at :259-276); rule_matches still reads an exception (:659-663), which only the frozen v1 table can now carry; the longest match at each start is _spans (:754-773); credit_matches with its longer-phrase filter is :776-796, its two-way exemption for cash paid-in wording and a payment-method cue :786-792 and :803-806; credit_verdict is :844-861, _classify_credit :911-941, reversal linking :948-989 and the round-trip pass :995-1019. At 2c72469 (the owner's review-22 fix, 5c9a5bd, and D137's check, neither yet reviewed) the lines move by 22 from :279, by 29 from :462 and by 43 after credit_matches: validate_ruleset is :378-532, refusing an anchor on every v2 pattern but the bank's code (:462-468, with _has_anchor at :279-298); _spans is :783-803; credit_matches is :805-839, which settles which rules count by repeating its filter (:815-839); credit_verdict is :887-906, _classify_credit :954-986, reversal linking :1007-1032 and the round-trip pass :1038-1062. At 4b3b21c (the owner's review-23 fix, not yet reviewed) the lines up to credit_matches are as at 2c72469. credit_matches is :805-844 and settles occurrence by occurrence (:815-843), and every line after it moves by 5: credit_verdict is :892-911, _classify_credit :959-991, reversal linking :1012-1037 and the round-trip pass :1043-1067. At 9042fb4 (the owner's review-24 fix, not yet reviewed) validate_ruleset is :378-534 and applies every check a v2 table gets to every table but one that bears v1's id and calls itself frozen (:381-384), whose bytes load_ruleset compares with the hash pinned in code (:665-673); credit_matches is :809-849, credit_verdict :896-915, _classify_credit :963-995, reversal linking :1016-1046 with its bank prefixes at :1000-1002, and the round-trip pass :1047-1073. At 7cb2e71 (the owner's review-25 fix, not yet reviewed) _is_bank_code_text (:368-371) is the type test behind the two bank-code membership checks (:440, :479); validate_ruleset is :384-540, and still gathers the codes the bank-code rules carry by hashing each such rule's pattern (:534); load_ruleset is :667-694 and compares a pinned table's bytes before it parses the table (:676-691); credit_matches is :829-869, credit_verdict :916-935, _classify_credit :983-1015, reversal linking :1036-1066 with its bank prefixes at :1020-1022, and the round-trip pass :1067-1093. At 5139e22 (the owner's review-26 fix, not yet reviewed) validate_ruleset is :384-541, and gathers the codes the bank-code rules carry only from patterns that are text (:534-535). load_ruleset is :667-697. It refuses any failure in parsing or checking a changed v1 as a changed frozen table first (:685-688), and any other failure that is not the table's own refusal as a malformed value that could not be checked, naming no rule (:689-691). credit_matches starts at :832, credit_verdict at :919 (with the bank's code at step 2 at :925) and _classify_credit at :986, and the reversal-linking prefixes are at :1024. At 5f5bb2e (the owner's review-27 fix, not yet reviewed) validate_ruleset is :384-561. It takes the hash of the table's bytes and spares the exception checks only for v1 as its bytes were verified (:390-393, :449-474), keeps the checks only the maintained lists need for every table but one loaded as v1 that calls itself frozen (:475-505, :508-517, :538-543), and builds every well-typed rule's matchers while it checks the table (:544-557, with _well_typed_text and _build_matchers at :563-583), which ruleset_from_data reuses (:638-656). load_ruleset is :707-737. credit_matches starts at :872, credit_verdict at :959 (with the bank's code at step 2 at :965) and _classify_credit at :1026, and the reversal-linking prefixes are at :1064. At 4c017d4 (the owner's review-38 fix, not yet reviewed) validate_ruleset is :397-622. It spares the exception checks only where a digest that matches the pin is given (:403-407). It names a key given more than once and checks every rules list given (:415-423), and checks each value of a repeated field for exceptions, lookarounds and anchors (:474-500, with _variants at :794-803). It builds each rule's matchers part by part (:573-618). The loader keeps a repeated key as an unbuilt value holding every value given (_UniqueKeyLoader, :735-770; _Repeated, :786-791). ruleset_from_data is :700-728 and load_ruleset :868-898. credit_matches starts at :1056, credit_verdict at :1143 (with the bank's code at step 2 at :1149) and _classify_credit at :1210, and the reversal-linking prefixes are at :1248. At 3ae10cc (the owner's fix for verify-117, reviewed as verify-119) validate_ruleset is :419-691; the loader keeps a key given more than once as an unbuilt value holding every value given (_UniqueKeyLoader, :821-856, with _Repeated at :872-877); and load_ruleset (:960-989) turns any failure that is not the table's own refusal into one (:977-983). credit_matches starts at :1147, credit_verdict at :1234 and _classify_credit at :1301.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:447:| Genuine trading income separated from transfers, director money and cash | Revenue is claimed narrowly; every other credit, cash included, is one OTHER pile in M16 | No breakdown of money in; cash takings, director loans, own-account transfers and refunds look alike |
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:448:| Real acquirers and merchant cash advances recognised | Only synthetic acquirer names (README:125-126) | Real card revenue lands in M16 and refers the case |
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:470:**Sub-decision: how to show what is unexplained.** (i) Redefine M16 to mean unexplained only. (ii) Keep M16's value (money in that is not trading receipts), rename it to say so, and add a figure for each bucket plus the unexplained share (M26). *Chose (ii)*: a memo already signed that cites M16 must keep its meaning, and ids are stable.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:514:**Sub-decision: which category a credit gets inside one bucket (revision 4).** Two rules can agree on the bucket but carry different categories: a gateway (card settlement) and an invoice reference (customer transfer) are both trading receipts; a returned-payment cue and a refund cue are both refunds and returned payments. The category matters downstream: the round-trip check undoes only customer transfers, M15 reads customer transfers, M7, reversal linking and the returned-DD indicator read returned payments, the M16 numerator reads refunds, and M18 and the cash indicators read cash deposits. (i) The first rule in table order (revision 3): reordering rules then changes M2, M7 and M15, which the fifth review showed. (ii) Make it ambiguous: R4 defines ambiguity by bucket, so this would amend R4 and fill the queue with credits whose bucket is not in doubt. (iii) A category order declared with the table and applied once the bucket is known, putting first the category that brings more checks. *Chose (iii)*, which the owner confirmed (D61): in trading receipts, cash deposit, then customer transfer, then card settlement; in refunds and returned payments, returned payment, then refund. A payer cannot avoid the round-trip check by adding a gateway's name, or the cash flags by adding an invoice reference. What (iii) costs: a gateway payout that carries an invoice reference counts as a named customer in M15. The order is data in the table and is hashed with it.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:701:- A judgement queue in `cma run`'s terminal review (revision 4). `cma run` is not a case: under v2 it records each ambiguous credit's conflict and candidates in its evidence lines and counts the credit in M16, and the queue belongs to the workspace (R15). `cma run` has no profile, so its candidates never include a profile name, and no credit is ambiguous there because of one.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:774:  - M16 is renamed so it cannot be confused with M26 (unexplained); M18 is the cash inside M2, not a bucket beside it; [R1] to [R9] in a pack are policy rules, not requirement ids.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:796:- **Data-intensive.** *Idempotency:* imports are keyed by file hash, an identical override changes nothing, and a second sign-off is refused. *Determinism:* a credit's table verdict, category and table candidates are a pure function of its description, the sign of its amount and the tables with their category order. Its profile candidates, a profile settlement and a profile-made ambiguity add the case profile's names, which the snapshot hashes. Neither a credit's bucket nor its category depends on rule order within a stage or, since revision 10, on the order of alternatives inside a pattern; only which of several same-category rules is recorded, and the order candidates are written in, follow table order. A debit's category follows table order by design. *Ordering and concurrency:* one writer at a time per case, across windows, commands, threads and CaseLog objects in one process, on a local disk, while nobody removes or replaces the lock files, and one save at a time per holder; enforced by a gate, a lock, a save-in-progress mark and the expected log position. A second tab is refused while the first writes, and gets a conflict and reloads if it writes from a stale position. A reader holds the gate shared while it reads, so no writer takes the case during the read; it holds the case lock shared too when the case is free, and a writer waits for it. While another writer holds the case, a reader judges only the recorded entries, from a read the head record held still across. *Partial failure:* append-only logs with a check for a torn last entry; the head record is written before the first line, so a lost head record is never mistaken for a torn first append; a repair replaces the log in one rename; the pack is written to a temporary folder and then renamed. Two saves told recorded at one position under interference break the chain or the head record there. A step refused once any part of the line is written leaves an unrecorded entry, whole or torn, as a crash does, and the writer says the save was not recorded, never that nothing was written (revision 19). A step the file system fails answers the same way, naming the error, and one after the head record's rename, which cannot be made durable, answers not confirmed (revisions 18 and 19). *Consistency:* the workspace reads the latest state; the preview and sign-off bind to a snapshot that includes the ruleset, the profile, thresholds, the policy hash and the show_policy_verdict setting, so a sign-off cannot attach to an outcome the underwriter did not see. *Migration:* additive, nothing to backfill. *Money:* integer pence, GBP only, thresholds compared in pence, and amounts with more than two decimals are refused.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:820:**New figures.** No id is ever reused. M1-M16 keep their ids and units. M16 is renamed 'Non-trading money in (share)'; M2 keeps the name Total revenue, and its method sentence now names cash paid in. Values move only on lines a v2 rule now recognises or finds ambiguous (R10).
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:837:Reconciliation: M17 = M2 + M19 + M20 + M21 + M22 + M23 + M24 + M25, in pence. M18 is part of M2, is not added again, and its evidence is a subset of M2's. M16 and M26 share one denominator: money in less lending drawdowns, returned direct debits and own-account transfers. Cash deposits are trading receipts, so they are in neither numerator; an ambiguous credit is unexplained, so it is in both, whatever profile name it carries as a candidate. Credits that fall to OTHER today keep the same treatment in M9 whichever new bucket they land in, with one exception: cash deposits now count as trading cash (D31). No committed fixture or synthetic case has a cash line (Consumer trace, item 13), so no committed value moves because of cash. M27 averages the closing balance of every calendar day in the period, carried over days with no lines, as M6 does. M28 adds the proposed monthly repayment for each month of the period to existing debt service.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:845:**Policy check.** PolicyResult and policy.evaluate do not change. The workspace and the pack evaluate only the packaged policy. The label is one constant with no digits in it, so it never trips the guard: 'Illustrative thresholds shipped with this tool. They are not your firm's lending policy, and this is not a lending decision: the underwriter decides.' The pack's evidence records whether the check was shown, the label, and the policy's name and hash. These do not go into PolicyResult, so the checkpoint allow-list and `cma run` are untouched. As in revision 1, the description of policy rule R9 is reworded for M16's new name, and the policy hash changes.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:932:**Graph and prompt.** Unchanged, except that the metrics table has more rows and M16 has its new name. FakeLLM parses rows by id and is unaffected; its sentence about M16 is reworded. Under v2 the evidence lines `cma run` writes carry each line's rule, the conflict form included; the terminal review shows no queue.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1009:| `cma run` meets an ambiguous credit under v2 | Its evidence line records the conflict and every table candidate, and the credit counts in M16; `cma run` has no profile, so no profile candidate and no profile-made ambiguity; the terminal review shows no queue (out of scope; the queue is the workspace's) | R4, R53 |
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1091:## Migration
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1094:- Evidence JSON v1 is a superset of today's record. Records without schema_version are read as v0; verify recomputes M1-M16 for them and says that is all it can check.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1095:- The committed examples (examples/cafe_stretched.memo.md, .memo.evidence.json and .truth.json) are regenerated in the same change. The CSV is byte-identical; the appendix gains M17-M28, M16's new name and M2's reworded method; in the truth file, the director capital line becomes director_funding. test_categoriser_matches_truth runs under v2; v1 is covered by P7, which compares values.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1123:- Policy YAML: R9's description (uk_sme_default.yaml:86, 'left unexplained') is reworded to match M16's new name, and the policy hash changes, recorded as before. R9 keeps M16. Whether it should move to M26 is a decision for whoever owns the firm's policy. The workspace and pack read the same packaged file, so there is no second policy to migrate.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1230:3. **Metric list from metrics.compute (M17-M28 added, M16 renamed, M2's method reworded).** `rg -n 'evidence_record|load_csv|met[.]compute|metrics[.]compute|as_table|categorise[.]categorise|cat[.]categorise|Metric[(]' -g '!.bridgie/**'`. Consumers: src/cma/graph.py (draft table, appendix, evidence_record), src/cma/cli.py (_print_metrics), src/cma/mcp_server.py (compute_metrics, check_policy), src/cma/policy.py (checks metric ids), src/cma/guard.py (tags against metrics), src/cma/llm.py (FakeLLM parses table rows), evals/run_evals.py (truth by id), tests/test_metrics.py, tests/test_panel_run02_metrics.py, tests/test_panel_run03.py, tests/test_categorise.py, tests/test_import_monzo.py, tests/test_guard.py (builds Metric objects), and the committed examples. Effect: additive; the regex `rg -n 'findall' src/cma/llm.py` (lines 36 and 38) keys on table rows and still matches.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1231:4. **M16's name and its share denominator.** `rg -n -i 'unclassified' -g '!.bridgie/**'`; `rg -n 'unexplained' src/cma/policies`. Consumers: src/cma/metrics.py:289, src/cma/llm.py:56, src/cma/categorise.py:12 (docstring), src/cma/synth.py (truth field), src/cma/policies/uk_sme_default.yaml:86 (R9 description), README.md:69,118,124, evals/run_evals.py:100, tests/test_metrics.py:53, tests/test_import_monzo.py:63-65, tests/test_panel_run02_metrics.py:73, tests/test_panel_run03.py:130, examples/cafe_stretched.memo.md:5,44,56, examples/cafe_stretched.memo.evidence.json, examples/cafe_stretched.truth.json. Effect: rename and wording only. Values change only where own-account lines now leave the denominator, cash lines leave the numerator, or ambiguous credits stay in it; those fixtures are listed in the change, and today none has a cash line.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1246:19. **Category inside a bucket (declared order).** `git grep -n -E 'REVENUE_TRANSFER|RETURNED_DD|CASH_DEPOSIT|REFUND_IN' refs/heads/bridgie-task/statement-review/T03 -- 'src/**' 'evals/**'` at ecec4ca: src/cma/categorise.py:402 (the rail-plus-cash check), :497 and :504 (reversal linking), :532 (the round-trip check); src/cma/metrics.py:30 (the M16 numerator), :156 and :176 (M7 and reversals), :207 (M15), :247; src/cma/models.py:19, 29, 31, 34, 56-62 and 68 (the categories, the bucket map, REVENUE_CATEGORIES); src/cma/synth.py:185, 235, 280 and 287 (truth). In the T03 worktree at 2c72469 (five lines later at 4b3b21c), reversal linking is src/cma/categorise.py:1007-1032, which since D119 looks debits up by amount and payee, reading the payee through counterparty() and its fixed prefix list (:991-1004), which revision 10 widens under v2 to the returned-payment wording the table recognises (not yet built). Effect: v2 only. A credit with a gateway name and an invoice reference becomes a customer transfer, and one with both returned-payment and refund wording a returned payment. The bucket map does not change, v1 keeps first-match order and its reversal links, and the generator's existing credits each carry wording from one list at most (Migration), so their truth does not move.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:1262:    On the integration branch at 4b4030b, at 6573a9d for revision 16 and at 4709d5f for revision 17, the same pattern finds nothing, and so do _has_lookaround, _rail_and_cash, _has_anchor, survivors and inside_longer. Effect: the flag is part of the v2 table and its hash, and a table edit that moves it, drops it or narrows it is refused at load. The bank's code is exempt from the longer-phrase filter, and each part's longest end counts. Both change only the v2 categoriser's matching, so no reader outside categorise.py changes, and no shipped verdict moves (Migration). Since D110 (ef057ad) the validator also refuses a v2 pattern with a repeat that can match across a space, and since D117 to D121 atomic groups, possessive repeats, patterns that can match no text, and ids that are reserved or hold anything but letters, digits, _, . and - (:356-501 at 78556a7). The ten shipped patterns pass. Since 4adddf9 it refuses an `unless` or a `requires` on every v2 rule (:423-432 at 78556a7; D126, R4 since revision 14), and since 78556a7 a lookahead or lookbehind in a v2 pattern (_has_lookaround, :259-276, checked at :433-439; D136, R4 since revision 15), with four cases in one test (tests/test_rules_v2.py:1862-1870); rule_matches still reads an exception (:659-663), for the frozen v1 table. Since 78556a7 the exemption in the longer-phrase filter runs both ways: cash paid-in wording and a payment-method cue never count as inside each other's phrase (:786-792, with _rail_and_cash at :803-806; D132), with a test for each side (tests/test_rules_v2.py:1815-1823 and :1851-1859). An anchored v2 pattern still loads there (revision 15's question). At 2c72469 (5c9a5bd and D137's check, neither yet reviewed) the same pattern finds 57 matches in the same three files (src/cma/categorise.py 34, src/cma/rules/v2.yaml 1, tests/test_rules_v2.py 22). Its lines move from 78556a7's by 22 from :279, by 29 from :462 and by 43 after credit_matches: validate_ruleset is :378-532, with the anchor refusal at :462-468 (_has_anchor, :279-298; D137, R4 since revision 16), credit_matches is :805-839, whose filter now lets only a match of a rule that still counts set another aside, settled by repeating it (:815-839; D141, R4 since revision 16), and the bank's code at step 2 is :893. The anchor refusal has five cases in one test (tests/test_rules_v2.py:1896) and the set-aside rule one test (:1879). At 4b3b21c (the owner's review-23 fix, not yet reviewed) the same pattern finds 57 matches in the same three files (src/cma/categorise.py 34, src/cma/rules/v2.yaml 1, tests/test_rules_v2.py 22). Its lines are as at 2c72469 up to credit_matches, which is :805-844 and settles occurrence by occurrence (:815-843; D142, R4 since revision 17). The lines after it move by 5, and the bank's code at step 2 is :898. Its test is tests/test_rules_v2.py:1910. At 9042fb4 (the owner's review-24 fix, not yet reviewed) the same pattern finds 57 matches in the same three files (src/cma/categorise.py 34, src/cma/rules/v2.yaml 1, tests/test_rules_v2.py 22). validate_ruleset is :378-534; the checks a v2 table gets now run on every table but one that bears v1's id and calls itself frozen (:381-384, used at :440, :497 and :527; D153, R4 since revision 18), and the bank's code at step 2 is :901. Its test is tests/test_rules_v2.py:1927. At 7cb2e71 (the owner's review-25 fix, not yet reviewed) the same pattern finds 59 matches in the same three files (src/cma/categorise.py 36, src/cma/rules/v2.yaml 1, tests/test_rules_v2.py 22). _is_bank_code_text (:368-371) is the type test behind the two bank-code membership checks (:440, :479); validate_ruleset is :384-540, the codes the bank-code rules carry still gathered by hashing their patterns (:534); and the bank's code at step 2 is :921. Its tests are tests/test_rules_v2.py:1942 and :1957. At 5139e22 (the owner's review-26 fix, not yet reviewed) the same pattern finds 63 matches in the same three files (src/cma/categorise.py 36, src/cma/rules/v2.yaml 1, tests/test_rules_v2.py 26). The codes the bank-code rules carry are gathered only from patterns that are text (:534-535; D163, R4 since revision 20), and the bank's code at step 2 is :925. Its tests are tests/test_rules_v2.py:1970 and :1982. At 5f5bb2e (the owner's review-27 fix, not yet reviewed) the same pattern finds 62 matches in the same three files (src/cma/categorise.py 35, src/cma/rules/v2.yaml 1, tests/test_rules_v2.py 26). validate_ruleset is :384-561. It takes the hash of the table's bytes and spares the exception checks only for v1 as its bytes were verified (:390-393, :449-474; D168, R4 since revision 21), and builds every well-typed rule's matchers while it checks the table (:544-557, with _well_typed_text and _build_matchers at :563-583), which ruleset_from_data reuses (:638-656); the bank's code at step 2 is :965. Its tests are tests/test_rules_v2.py:1999 and :2016. At 4c017d4 (the owner's review-38 fix, not yet reviewed) the same pattern finds 67 matches in the same three files (src/cma/categorise.py 35, src/cma/rules/v2.yaml 1, tests/test_rules_v2.py 31). validate_ruleset is :397-622. It spares the exception checks only where a digest that matches the pin is given (:403-407; D192, R4 since revision 22). It names a repeated rules key and checks every list given (:415-423), and checks each value of a repeated field for exceptions, lookarounds and anchors (:474-500, with _variants at :794-803). It compiles and builds only a repeated field's last value (:590-618). The bank's code at step 2 is :1149. Its tests for revision 22 are tests/test_rules_v2.py:2291, :2309 and :2319.
  1380	    return " ".join(d.split()) or description.upper().strip()
  1381	
  1382	
  1383	def link_reversals(statement: Statement) -> Statement:
  1384	    """Point each returned DD credit at the most recent unreversed debit it undoes.
  1385	
  1386	    Same amount, same payee, on or before the reversal date. A returned DD with no
  1387	    amount (some banks print a zero-value notice) or no matching debit stays unlinked:
  1388	    it still counts in M7 but changes no sum.
  1389	    """
  1390	    taken: set[str] = set()
  1391	    txns = statement.transactions
  1392	    # Debits by (amount in pence, payee), in statement order: each returned DD looks only
  1393	    # at the debits it could undo, latest first, not back through the whole statement.
  1394	    debits: dict[tuple[int, str], list[int]] = {}
